<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tristan und Isolde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1865</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tristan and Isolde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tristan und Isolde is an opera by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>, composed between 1857 and 1859 on the medieval legend of the lovers Tristan and Isolde. It was first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 10 June 1865 under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a>. Its harmonic language, beginning with the famous &quot;Tristan chord&quot;, had a profound influence on later music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tristan-und-isolde.jpg" alt="Tristan und Isolde" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josef Albert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Albert_-_Ludwig_und_Malwine_Schnorr_von_Carolsfeld_-_Tristan_und_Isolde,_1865f.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
